# D Flip-Flop (D FF) using Verilog HDL

## Overview

This project implements a **positive edge-triggered D Flip-Flop with an asynchronous active-high reset** using Verilog HDL. A testbench is included to verify its functionality through simulation.

## Objective

* Design a positive edge-triggered D Flip-Flop.
* Implement an asynchronous reset.
* Verify the circuit using a Verilog testbench.
* Observe the output waveform for different input conditions.

---

## Module Description

### `d_ff`

The `d_ff` module stores the value of input **D** on the rising edge of the clock (`clk`). When the reset (`rst`) is HIGH, the output is immediately cleared to `0`, regardless of the clock.

### Inputs

* `clk` – Clock signal
* `rst` – Asynchronous active-high reset
* `d` – Data input

### Output

* `q` – Stored output

### Working

* On every **positive edge of the clock**, the value of `d` is transferred to `q`.
* If `rst = 1`, the output `q` is immediately reset to `0`.
* The output retains its value until the next rising edge of the clock.

---

## Reset Logic

```verilog
if (rst)
    q <= 1'b0;
else
    q <= d;
```

* `rst = 1` → Output is cleared immediately.
* `rst = 0` → Output follows the value of `d` on the next positive clock edge.

---

## Testbench Description

### `tb_d_ff`

The testbench verifies the D Flip-Flop by generating a clock signal, applying different values to the data input, and testing the reset operation.

### Clock Generation

* Clock period = **10 ns**
* Rising edge every **5 ns**

```verilog
forever #5 clk = ~clk;
```

---

## Test Cases

| Time (ns) | Reset | D | Expected Q       |
| --------- | ----- | - | ---------------- |
| 0         | 1     | 0 | 0 (Reset active) |
| 10        | 0     | 1 | 1                |
| 20        | 0     | 0 | 0                |
| 30        | 0     | 1 | 1                |
| 40        | 1     | X | 0 (Reset active) |
| 50        | 0     | 0 | 0                |

---

## Simulation Monitoring

The `$monitor` statement continuously displays the signal values during simulation.

```verilog
$monitor("Time=%0t clk=%b rst=%b d=%b q=%b",
         $time, clk, rst, d, q);
```

This helps verify that the output changes only on the positive clock edge and resets immediately when `rst` becomes HIGH.

---

## Expected Behavior

| Reset | Clock Edge     | D | Q                    |
| ----- | -------------- | - | -------------------- |
| 1     | X              | X | 0                    |
| 0     | Rising Edge    | 0 | 0                    |
| 0     | Rising Edge    | 1 | 1                    |
| 0     | No Rising Edge | X | Holds previous value |

---

## Features

* Positive edge-triggered operation
* Asynchronous active-high reset
* Non-blocking assignments (`<=`)
* Simple and synthesizable Verilog design
* Easy to simulate in Vivado, ModelSim, or other Verilog simulators

---

## Applications

* Registers
* Shift registers
* Counters
* Data synchronization
* Memory elements
* FPGA and ASIC digital designs

---

## Simulation Result

The simulation verifies that:

* The output updates only on the **rising edge** of the clock.
* The **asynchronous reset** immediately forces the output to `0`.
* The D Flip-Flop correctly stores and holds the input data until the next clock edge.
